ad  the manga and saw deque who really  developed as a character but I was  fascinated by melioidosis character  right from the very beginning I loved  the twist at the end of the first  episode and the first chapter where we  learned that this small carefree guy  with an over-the-top sense of humor is  actually the captain of the seven deadly  sins instead of watching him go from an  underdog to a great warrior when we meet  Mele Oda's he is already one of the  greatest warriors in the world and yet  the seven deadly sins still manages to  be a great story that's because even  though the protagonist is overpowered  the story stays interesting the plot is  not about  watching the main character train and  groves step-by-step but rather it is  about a quest and the first important  quest is to reunite all the members of  the seven deadly sins since every member  of the seven deadly sins is very strong  it is still interesting to see all of  them interacting with Mellie Otis and I  especially loved the relationship  between Mellie Otis and bond the  interesting thing about Mellie Otis is  not seeing how he goes from weak to  strong but rather the mystery of how he  became so strong in the first place  nevertheless getting stronger or  powering up is an important shown intro  and the way that the seven deadly sins  includes this trope is by making Mellie  Otis a demon who at times loses control  of his power which makes him stronger  but also more dangerous on top of that  we later learned that the vast majority  of melioidosis power has been sealed  away in order to prevent him from losing  control and so once he retrieves this  power he is able to power up even more  you might think that having such an Opie  protagonist would make the show boring  since he'll have no trouble with any  villain but by adding this dimension  that meliodas has to fight against  himself and his own demonic nature and  by introducing extremely strong villains  later on in the series including the 10  commandments who had been sealed away in  the coffin of eternal darkness for 3,000  years the story of the seven deadly sins  stays interesting even though Mellie  Otis is incredibly strong as are the  other members of the seven deadly sins  they still face major challenges both in  terms of their own inner demons pun  intended and in terms of outside threats  like the ten commandments beyond just  Milly ODIs and deku the different  approaches to character development in  the two series follow a similar pattern  in boku no hero most of the main cast  are relatively normal high school  students so they're all young their  journey is just beginning and there  isn't too much that the series can do in  terms of complex backstory don't get me  wrong some of the characters do have  interesting backstories including show  Totoro ki in particular but most of the  main cast in the seven deadly sins are  much older the youngest members of the  seven deadly sins are in their 40s while  others are hundreds and even thousands  of years old so of course  the seven deadly sins has a lot more  room to explore the rich histories and  backstories of its characters whereas  boku no hero has to focus mostly on the  present that's not to say that one of  these approaches is good and the other  is bad they are just different I  absolutely love that in the seven deadly  sins a lot of time is spent on the  backstories of the characters which  gives us a better understanding of them  as people and allows us to see their  progression on the other hand in boku no  hero we get to see the personalities of  the characters being shaped in real time  in the present moment and this is  extremely compelling as well you feel  like you are growing up together with  these characters instead of getting to  know them when they are fully formed  adults which allows us to connect with  them on another level and this is what I  find so interesting these two series  take very different approaches to  character development including the  protagonist and main character himself  and yet I really enjoy both of them  because they are great at what they do  we are introduced to the important  characters in completely different ways  but in both cases the characters are  compelling enough for us to feel a  connection to them the story moves  forward without becoming stale because  of the excellent world building and  character development and whether we're  looking at the mystery of what happens  next to Deku and how he becomes the  greatest hero or the mystery of what  really happened in the holy war 3,000  years ago in each case the mystery draws  us in and keeps us guessing the manga  for both series is far ahead of the  anime and as I am caught up to both of  them I can tell you that neither series  has gotten less interesting as time goes  on if anything both manga have gotten  better after the events that are covered  in the first season of the anime and I  can't wait to see how some of the  upcoming arcs are going to be adapted in  anime form but that is all from me on  this topic let me know what you guys  think do you enjoy both of these series  despite the fact that they are so  different and do you happen to prefer  one over the other if so let me know why  down below I always love hearing from  you don't forget to check out nock stock  whose new video comparing Mellie Otis  and Kirito as I said we worked on both  of these videos together so his video is  like a companion video to this one if  you enjoyed this type of comparison  video and you want to see more in the  future be sure to leave a like to let me  know if you are new to the channel don't  forget to subscribe and Detroit smash  that notification bell and be sure to  check us out on twitter and instagram at  anime uproar as always thank you all so  much for watching and until next time  see us space cowboys  [Music]  [Applause]  you  [Music]

